e (permission denied, exit 126) and a device node created in /tmp could not be read via dd (permission denied), both before and after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6bc1fbc15f084121\",\"cmd-9384549c56b94813\",\"cmd-3f2fd685f0b94bb2\",\"cmd-069c2781fe7b444d\"]},{\"id\":\"devshm-mount-options\",\"requirement\":\"/dev/shm is mounted with noexec, nosuid, and nodev enforced\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline showed /dev/shm had only nosuid,nodev (no noexec). After remounting with the new fstab options, findmnt confirmed rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec on /dev/shm, and a mknod'd device node in /dev/shm could not be read, both pre- and post-reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed14ff6a083049a6\",\"cmd-6bc1fbc15f084121\",\"cmd-9384549c56b94813\",\"cmd-069c2781fe7b444d\"]},{\"id\":\"persist-reboot\",\"requirement\":\"Settings survive a reboot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Persistent /etc/fstab entries were added for /tmp and /dev/shm with defaults,noexec,nosuid,nodev. A genuine reboot was performed (boot_id changed from 901ef286-... to cfe6cf8b-..., uptime reset, node briefly unreachable during the transition), and post-reboot findmnt plus repeated exec/device-node falsification tests confirmed the same enforced options were active after restart, directly demonstrating the pre-reboot healthy state, the disruption, and the recovered state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9fd021acefce4499\",\"cmd-ed69cf6dff63471a\",\"cmd-35b25889f0f143fb\",\"cmd-9c2be3392e954c8f\",\"cmd-1263954de1fa401d\",\"cmd-069c2781fe7b444d\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map clearly to the task: fstab backup+edit (cmd-890beaa1d3a84032), live remount to apply changes (cmd-6bc1fbc15f084121), falsification test artifacts in /tmp and /dev/shm created to prove enforcement (cmd-9384549c56b94813, cmd-069c2781fe7b444d) and immediately removed in the same or following command (cmd-3f2fd685f0b94bb2, cmd-069c2781fe7b444d). The reboot itself (cmd-ed69cf6dff63471a) was necessary to prove persistence and used a bounded, self-cleaning background delay. The only lasting artifact is the /etc/fstab.bak.<timestamp> backup file, a low-risk, standard safety practice before editing a system config file, not unwanted residue. Lifecycle diff shows no failed services, no unexpected listening sockets (only PID/port churn from the reboot itself), and clean package health before and after.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-890beaa1d3a84032\",\"cmd-6bc1fbc15f084121\",\"cmd-9384549c56b94813\",\"cmd-3f2fd685f0b94bb2\",\"cmd-069c2781fe7b444d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly configured /etc/fstab with persistent tmpfs mounts for /tmp and /dev/shm using noexec,nosuid,nodev, applied them live, and directly proved enforcement via falsification tests (blocked script execution, blocked device-node read) both before and after a genuine, evidenced reboot (boot_id change, unreachability window, post-restart verification). All three material outcomes are satisfied with direct, non-configuration-only evidence. Operational hygiene is high: all mutations were purposeful and test artifacts were cleaned up; only a standard fstab backup file was left behind.\",\"limitations\":[\"Evidence is limited to the specific falsification tests chosen (script execution, character device read via mknod+dd); other setuid-bit behaviors (e.g., an actual setuid binary invocation) were not separately tested, though nosuid was confirmed via findmnt mount options in both baseline and post-reboot checks.\",\"No evidence was provided for whether the /etc/fstab.bak backup file remains present long-term, though this is expected low-risk residue from a standard safety practice.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"tmp-mount-options","requirement":"/